// hyphenation-demo — the bundled hyphenation patterns, language by language.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/hyphenation-demo.script
//
// Hyphenation is what lets a justified paragraph keep even spacing at a narrow
// measure: without it the breaker can only stretch the spaces between whole
// words, and a long word arriving at the wrong moment leaves a line full of
// gaps. texish breaks words with Liang's algorithm, the one TeX uses, from the
// same pattern files a TeX installation loads.
//
// Every language here is one file, taken verbatim from hyph-utf8. Five of them
// — en-us, es, fr, it and pt — are compiled into the binary, so they work on an
// installation with nothing on disk. The rest are files in the hyphenation/
// folder an installation ships, and \usehyphenation reaches both the same way:
// a document names a language and does not care which tier it is in.
//
// A pattern file says more than which letters may be split. It carries an
// exception list for the words its patterns get wrong, and the two minima its
// language is set with — English asks for three letters after a break, which is
// why "comput-er" appears in no English book.

\use{document}

\title{Hyphenation in \TeXish}
\author{The texish engine}
\maketitle

\section{Why a narrow measure needs it}

Both columns below set the same paragraph at the same measure. The left one has no
language selected, so the breaker may only stretch the spaces between whole words; the
right one hyphenates.

\medskip

\set hsize {200}
\setbox plain \vtop{\noindent Extraordinarily complicated typographical arrangements demonstrate the difficulty
immediately: unbreakable words accumulate and the interword spaces stretch to
accommodate them.}
\usehyphenation{en-us}
\setbox broken \vtop{\noindent Extraordinarily complicated typographical arrangements demonstrate the difficulty
immediately: unbreakable words accumulate and the interword spaces stretch to
accommodate them.}
\set hsize {460}

\noindent\hbox{\box plain \hskip 30 \box broken}

\section{Languages compiled into the binary}

These five need no files on disk, which is what makes them work on a binary somebody
downloaded and put in a folder of its own.

\medskip

\set hsize {136}
\usehyphenation{fr}
\setbox french \vtop{\noindent La miséricorde et la résurrection sont des mots que la bibliothèque française coupe
volontiers, même dans une colonne étroite.}
\usehyphenation{es}
\setbox spanish \vtop{\noindent La misericordia y la resurrección son palabras que la biblioteca española divide sin
dificultad, incluso en una columna estrecha.}
\usehyphenation{pt}
\setbox portuguese \vtop{\noindent A misericórdia e a ressurreição são palavras que a biblioteca portuguesa divide
naturalmente, mesmo numa coluna estreita.}
\set hsize {460}

\noindent\hbox{\box french \hskip 20 \box spanish \hskip 20 \box portuguese}

\section{Languages that come from the pattern tree}

These are files in the installation's \mono{hyphenation} folder. On an installation
without one this page does not render at all, which is the point: the engine says the
folder is missing rather than quietly setting the paragraph unhyphenated.

\medskip

\set hsize {136}
\usehyphenation{de-1996}
\setbox german \vtop{\noindent Die Silbentrennung deutscher Wörter verlangt Trennmuster, und Zusammensetzungen
verlangen sie ganz besonders.}
\usehyphenation{nl}
\setbox dutch \vtop{\noindent De Nederlandse woordafbreking gebruikt patronen die verschillen van de Duitse en de
Engelse woordafbreking.}
\usehyphenation{pl}
\setbox polish \vtop{\noindent Polskie dzielenie wyrazów wykorzystuje wzorce przygotowane specjalnie dla języka
polskiego.}
\set hsize {460}

\noindent\hbox{\box german \hskip 20 \box dutch \hskip 20 \box polish}

\section{What a pattern file settles besides the patterns}

English is set with two letters before a break and three after --- both stated in the
file's own header --- so \mono{comput-er} is offered by the patterns and refused by the
language. The file also carries an exception list, which settles the handful of words
the patterns get wrong: \mono{as-so-ciate}, and \mono{project}, which is never broken
at all.

\medskip

\usehyphenation{en-us}
\set hsize {300}
\setbox english \vtop{\noindent The association projected a reciprocity that the declination of the manuscript could
not reasonably accommodate, however extraordinarily the philanthropic reformation was
presented.}
\set hsize {460}

\noindent\hbox{\box english}
